WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.160
Cutting-edge effects technology isn't always
used to create cool explosions and giant robots.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.060
Released nearly 30 years after the original
film, Tron: Legacy is a computerized adventure

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.760
packed with light cycles and Daft Punk music.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.100
Most importantly, this 2010 Disney film gave
audiences double the Dude.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:21.760
Jeff Bridges returned to the Tron universe
as two separate characters.

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:25.800
First, there's the heroic Kevin Flynn, who
looks exactly like the older actor.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.869
But then there's Clu, the artificial intelligence
gone rogue.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:33.180
And while he was played by a man in his 60s,
this evil computer program looks more or less

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.170
like Bridges did back in the '80s.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:38.771
VFX supervisor Eric Barba oversaw the transformation.

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:42.770
He achieved it with a four-camera, head-mounted
system that Bridges wore on the set to get

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.990
a floating, 3D point cloud of his face.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:49.590
The effect may look a little "computery,"
but that's okay because, after all, Clu is

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.580
a computer program.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:56.579
The tragic death of Paul Walker in 2013 sent
a massive shockwave through the Fast and Furious

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:57.080
community.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.460
After all, this is a franchise about family,
and both Walker and his character of Brian

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:04.340
O'Conner were super important to a whole lot
of people.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.450
His death had serious implications for the
production of Furious 7, as the actor died

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.140
during filming.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.780
Fortunately, Weta Digital was able to save
the day and preserve Walker's legacy for Fast

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.360
and Furious fans around the world.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:20.740
The folks at Weta digitally created a whopping
350 shots of Walker doing everything from

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.080
jumping skyscrapers to standing in a lineup
with the rest of his crew.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.480
Additionally, Walker's brothers, Caleb and
Cody, and actor John Brotherton gave motion

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.520
capture performances that Weta used to recreate
Brian.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.360
Thanks to their incredible work, he got to
ride with his family one last time.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.320
When Kevin Spacey was accused by multiple
victims of sexual assault, director Ridley

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.440
Scott made one of the gutsiest decisions in
Hollywood history.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.360
Spacey and Scott had recently finished working
on All the Money in the World when the scandal

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:47.860
broke.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.240
Unwilling to keep Spacey in the film, Scott
replaced the actor with Christopher Plummer,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.950
reshooting every scene featuring Spacey's
character, stingy billionaire J. Paul Getty.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:01.920
It was an unprecedented move, and it cost
$10 million to exorcise Spacey from the film.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.180
Most of these scenes were done practically,
with other actors on actual sets, but there

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.560
is one quick moment in which Scott resorted
to some digital trickery.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.700
For a scene in which Getty goes to Jordan,
it was too impractical to go all the way back

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.700
to the Middle East.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.750
So Plummer was shot in front of a green screen,
and after the special effects crew digitally

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.800
erased Spacey, they just slid Plummer right
into place.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:28.320
2017's Blade Runner 2049 was a beautiful follow-up
to the original 1982 Blade Runner.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:32.800
It saw the return of Harrison Ford as Rick
Deckard, but he wasn't the only original cast

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:33.560
member to come back.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.000
During one key scene, the aging Deckard is
confronted by a ghost from his past.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.940
It's Rachael, the replicant he fell in love
with in the first film.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.260
Only this isn't the Rachael that Deckard remembers.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.940
That Rachael is dead.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.130
This is a new, shiny one, created solely to
tempt Deckard to the dark side.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.960
And this isn't exactly the actress we remember,
either.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.830
In order to de-age Sean Young by over 30 years,
the Moving Picture Company analyzed photos

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.950
of her, studied scenes from the first film,
and even checked out Young's other performances

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.280
from around the same era.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.140
They also photographed Young with a special
device that captured her likeness from multiple

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:06.640
angles.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.000
With all these references, they were able
to create a CGI face they could then layer

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.260
onto a body double, who shot the scene with
Ford, with Young standing by as an adviser.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:20.720
Unlike many digital creations, the Rachael
of 2049 looks incredibly lifelike, which is

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.460
kind of perfect for a movie that wonders what
makes us human.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.580
In this film about adult friends playing a
game of Tag for years and years, Jeremy Renner

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.880
is an uncatchable man.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.960
He's been evading his buddies by moving like
an Olympic athlete and dodging outstretched

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.260
hands like he's in the Matrix.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.620
During one of these escape scenes, Renner
jumped on top of a huge stack of chairs, which

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.680
resulted in disaster when the rigging that
held the chairs in place snapped and sent

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.220
him tumbling.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.420
When Renner got up, he realized he'd broken
both his arms.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.720
As he explained to Entertainment Weekly, he
could still move his limbs "up and down kind

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.880
of like a robot," so he didn't need too much
digital assistance.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:00.070
However, there is one moment when the VFX
crew had to create a completely CG arm, and

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:04.230
it's in the very scene where his stunt went
wrong, specifically when he tosses a donut

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.340
at Ed Helms.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.070
Needless to say, we're legitimately impressed
that Renner could deal with all that pain

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:09.910
and continue filming.

